From: NAVEENKUMARKR777 <naveenkumarkr555@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 12:35:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 9593: Fix x86-64 segment-override prefix priority
 (CS/SS/DS/ES vs FS/GS)

In 64-bit mode, the CS/SS/DS/ES segment-override prefixes have no
effect and are treated as null prefixes for purposes of the
multiple-prefix rules (AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual, Vol. 2,
section on segment-override prefixes; consistent with Intel SDM Vol.
1 section 3.7.4.1, which forces the CS/DS/ES/SS segment bases to zero
in 64-bit mode) -- only FS and GS actually change which segment is
accessed. Sleigh tracked the active override in a single `segover`
context variable that any of these prefix bytes overwrote
unconditionally, so a CS/SS/DS/ES byte occurring closer to the opcode
than an earlier FS/GS override would silently clobber it, e.g.
`64 26 01 18` (fs; es; add [rax],ebx) was shown as
`ADD dword ptr ES:[RAX],EBX` instead of `FS:[RAX]`.

This is more than cosmetic: FS/GS-relative addressing is how x86-64
code reaches thread-local storage and the Windows PEB/TEB, and a
single redundant prefix byte was enough to hide that access from the
disassembly.

Add higher-priority 64-bit-only constructors so a CS/SS/DS/ES byte
becomes a true no-op when an FS/GS override is already active, instead
of overwriting it. 32-bit mode (where all six overrides are
meaningful and "last prefix wins" is correct) is untouched.

Verified against the byte sequences from the linked issue using the
real Sleigh compiler/disassembly engine, confirmed 32-bit mode and the
unrelated Jcc branch-hint use of the same context variable are
unaffected, and confirmed a byte-for-byte identical disassembly of
400K+ real instructions extracted from a live binary before/after the
change.

Fixes one of the still-open issues from #2033.
---
 Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc b/Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc
index fb267f7179..83585866a6 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc
+++ b/Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc
@@ -2399,6 +2399,22 @@ macro newpos2(pos1, pos2, off) {
 
 # The base level constructors
 #   The prefixes
+@ifdef IA64
+# In 64-bit mode the CS/SS/DS/ES segment override prefixes are null prefixes: they are
+# still valid bytes to encounter (and are reused elsewhere, e.g. as branch hints or
+# mandatory prefixes), but they must not change which segment is actually accessed.
+# Without the four guards below, a CS/SS/DS/ES byte occurring closer to the opcode than
+# an earlier FS/GS override would incorrectly clobber that FS/GS override, since segover
+# only ever remembers the most recently matched prefix (Intel SDM Vol. 2A section 2.1.1).
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x2e & segover=5; instruction [ segover=5; ] {} # CS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; FS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x2e & segover=6; instruction [ segover=6; ] {} # CS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; GS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x36 & segover=5; instruction [ segover=5; ] {} # SS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; FS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x36 & segover=6; instruction [ segover=6; ] {} # SS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; GS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x3e & segover=5; instruction [ segover=5; ] {} # DS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; FS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x3e & segover=6; instruction [ segover=6; ] {} # DS override has no effect in 64-bit mode; GS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x26 & segover=5; instruction [ segover=5; ] {} # ES override has no effect in 64-bit mode; FS override stays in effect
+:^instruction is $(LONGMODE_ON) & instrPhase=0 & over=0x26 & segover=6; instruction [ segover=6; ] {} # ES override has no effect in 64-bit mode; GS override stays in effect
+@endif
 :^instruction is instrPhase=0 & over=0x2e; instruction     [ segover=1; ]  {} # CS override
 :^instruction is instrPhase=0 & over=0x36; instruction     [ segover=2; ]  {} # SS override
 :^instruction is instrPhase=0 & over=0x3e; instruction     [ segover=3; ]  {} # DS override
